(1a) Is completely trivial. Follow the Solved Exam and use the same format.=
 Look at the top part of the Counting Notes where C(n, k) is defined if k >=
 n.

(1b) Trivial. Follow the Solved Exam.

(2a) Trivial. Please use parentheses if one of your numbers has two digits;=
 for instance, 3 and 12. In this case, write words as 3(12), (12)(12) as re=
quired so that it is clear that (12) is being treated as a single letter.

(2b) Trivial. Follow the Solved Exam.

(3a) Completely trivial. Follow the Solved Exam.

(3b) Completely trivial. Follow the Solved Exam.

(1c), (2c), (3c) are related and requires one to prove something directly a=
nd also by induction. The recursive definition MUST have the requisite numb=
er of cases and must use quantification properly. Otherwise one will get 0.=
  The direct prove must be done very carefully using natural deduction. The=
 inductive proof must be done using the induction template as in the Notes.=
 Please look at the Sets Notes also.

